<p>Tests are part of the language: a <code>test</code> block, an <code>assert</code> statement, and <code>bayan test</code>.</p>

<h2 id="unit-tests">Write and run unit tests</h2>
<p><strong>Runnable.</strong> A <code>test "name" { … }</code> block sits in the same file or a sibling <code>x.test.byn</code>. <code>assert</code> is a statement, not a function; a failure prints the value of every sub-expression. There is no assertion library; plain expressions cover it. <code>assert f() fails E.case</code> checks the error without a <code>catch</code> and a <code>panic</code>. <code>@cases(…)</code> turns one test into a table: each row runs and is reported separately. Failing functions are errors as values, so tests call them with <code>try</code>. <code>bayan run</code> ignores tests; <code>bayan test</code> runs them.</p>
<example name="cookbook-testing-unit"/>
<p>Running <code>bayan test cookbook-testing-unit.byn</code> prints:</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="bayan test">running 5 tests in 1 module

◆ 5 passed · 4.19s</code></pre>
<p>Change <code>assert n == 50</code> to <code>assert n == 51</code> and the failure shows the values:</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="bayan test">  ✗ t2b: accepts the middle
      assert n == 51
             │ │
             │ false
             50
      cookbook-testing-unit.byn:12

◆ 4 passed · ✗ 1 failed · 0 pending · 3.13s
  rerun the failure: bayan test --only "accepts the middle"</code></pre>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Python">def test_check_percent():
    with pytest.raises(RangeError):
        check_percent(101)</code></pre>
<p>Spec §20 lists <code>@pairwise</code>, <code>property</code>, <code>fuzz</code>, snapshots and doctests, all Planned; doctests are "Not in v0.1". <code>bayan test --help</code> shows what your build runs.</p>

<h2 id="benchmark">Benchmark a block of code</h2>
<p><strong>Planned</strong> for <code>bench</code> (§20, "Not in v0.1"): the spec form reports time and allocations per operation, and variance:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">bench "parse ledger" { measure Ledger.read(sample) }</code></pre>
<p><strong>Runnable today:</strong> time a loop by hand with <code>Io.monotonicNanos()</code> and divide by the runs. One loop has no warm-up or variance, so compare orders of magnitude only.</p>
<example name="cookbook-testing-bench"/>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Go">func BenchmarkCollatz(b *testing.B) {
    for i := 0; i &lt; b.N; i++ { collatzSteps(27) }
}</code></pre>
